index 0000000..c43f63b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/SOLUTION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Solution
+
+Two bugs in `lrucache/cache.py`, both in the recency bookkeeping.
+
+## 1. `get` did not count as a "use"
+
+`get` returned the value without reordering the `OrderedDict`, so a key's
+position only ever reflected when it was *written*, never when it was *read*.
+That makes the cache least-recently-**inserted** rather than least-recently-used:
+reading `"a"` then inserting a new key still evicted `"a"`. Fixed by calling
+`self._data.move_to_end(key)` on a successful (non-expired) lookup, matching the
+docstring's "Both `get` and `put` count as 'use'".
+
+## 2. `put` evicted on updates to existing keys
+
+The capacity check `if len(self._data) >= self.capacity` ran unconditionally, so
+overwriting a key that was *already present* first popped the LRU entry. Since
+the following assignment reused the existing slot rather than adding one, the
+cache silently shrank and an unrelated key was lost — `put("y", 3)` on a full
+cache destroyed `"x"`. Fixed by only evicting when the key is genuinely new
+(`if key not in self._data and len(...) >= self.capacity`).
+
+`python3 -m pytest -q` now passes (6 passed).
